(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-research-trials-quiz"]={"title":"Research and Trials Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is a placebo?","choices":["Looks identical but has no specific action","A weaker dose of the active treatment given openly","A standard therapy compared against the new one","A treatment that mimics the disease being studied"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it is indistinguishable from the treatment yet has no specific mechanism of action, which is what allows it to help blinding. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does the sample size of a non-inferiority trial usually compare with that of a superiority trial?","choices":["Smaller, because the comparator is established","The same, because the design is identical","Not needed, because no difference is sought","Larger, because differences may be small"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: because the differences to be ruled out may be small, non-inferiority trials usually require a larger sample size than superiority trials. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which term describes how correct a trial's results are for the patients it actually enrolled?","choices":["Generalizability","Internal validity","Compliance","Representativeness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it differs from generalizability, which is the degree results can be applied to other settings. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is confounding?","choices":["A factor whose presence changes the effect of exposure","Random error in measuring the outcome","An additional factor related to exposure and outcome","A participant switching between treatments"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: an additional independent factor related to both the exposure and the outcome, which can distort the apparent effect of the exposure. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why might a new treatment be tested for non-inferiority?","choices":["It may be cheaper, safer or easier","It is expected to be far more effective","It has no established comparison treatment","It avoids the need for randomization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: if it is unlikely to be less effective but has such advantages, it can be preferable to the established treatment. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a variable called when its presence or absence changes the exposure's effect?","choices":["A confounder","A selection factor","An effect modifier","A crossover variable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the exposure acts differently depending on the variable, which is not the same as merely distorting the association. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Besides statistical significance, what should a reader look for in a treatment study?","choices":["Clinical significance","A larger number of statistical tests","Agreement with the sponsor's expectations","The names of the participating centers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a difference can be statistically significant yet too small to matter to patients, so clinical significance must be judged as well. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is an advantage of observational studies of treatment?","choices":["They eliminate confounding","They include real-world patients","They assign treatment by chance","They guarantee balanced groups"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though the same feature carries drawbacks, since real-world patients differ in ways that are hard to control. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a trial, 21 of 150 patients given a new drug and 42 of 150 given standard care had the outcome. What is the risk in the new-drug group?","choices":["28 percent","14 percent","21 percent","50 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: 21 patients with the outcome divided by 150 in the group is 0.14, which is the risk of the outcome in that treatment group. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Of 9,000 patients screened, 60 percent meet the first criterion, half of those meet the second, and 80 percent of those meet the third. How many remain eligible?","choices":["5,400","2,700","7,200","2,160"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 9,000 x 0.6 = 5,400, then x 0.5 = 2,700, then x 0.8 = 2,160, a steady shrinking of the eligible pool. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In a trial, 20 of the 90 treated patients who completed follow-up had the outcome, and 10 of the original 100 treated patients were lost. Assuming every lost patient had the outcome, what is the treated group's risk?","choices":["22 percent","20 percent","30 percent","10 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the 10 lost patients are added to the 20 events, giving 30 out of the original 100 treated patients. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A drug's side effect occurs in about 1 in 20,000 patients. Which stage is most likely to detect it?","choices":["Phase I dose-finding studies","Postmarketing surveillance","Phase II effect-finding studies","Phase III randomized trials"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: following large numbers of patients after the drug is released is what helps identify more uncommon side effects. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In a trial of a new asthma inhaler, which is an exclusion criterion?","choices":["Allergy to a component of the inhaler","A confirmed diagnosis of asthma","Frequent symptoms despite treatment","Willingness to be randomized"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a contraindication to a treatment under evaluation is an exclusion criterion, a factor that prevents inclusion. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which factors does effectiveness take into account?","choices":["Compliance, practicality and cost","Dose, half-life and mechanism of action","Randomization, blinding and allocation","Age, sex and diagnostic category"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: these real-world factors are what separate effectiveness, in ordinary circumstances, from efficacy, in ideal circumstances. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which is a limitation of randomized controlled trials?","choices":["They cannot include a control group","They are expensive","They cannot be blinded","They cannot evaluate efficacy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they also raise logistical issues in sample selection, randomization, blinding, consistency across participants and time. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What should treatment decisions rest on, rather than coincidence, biased comparisons and wishful thinking?","choices":["The treatment most commonly used in practice","Impressions from memorable individual patients","Agreement among clinicians who use the treatment","Evidence on how treatments relate to outcomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because coincidence, biased comparisons and wishful thinking all mislead; evidence on the relationship between a treatment and its outcomes is the sound basis. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which stage of clinical trials is larger than phases I and II, randomized, and gives definitive evidence of effect?","choices":["Phase I","Phase II","Phase III","Postmarketing surveillance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it enrolls more participants, is randomized, gives definitive evidence of effect plus some common side effects, and is often published. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which is the best way to obtain a representative sample?","choices":["Random sampling","Enrolling the first volunteers who respond","Choosing patients the investigator judges typical","Selecting only patients from one clinic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: because all members of the population have the same chance of being selected, the sample tends to represent the population. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which is an example of cluster sampling?","choices":["All patients in designated hospital units","Every tenth patient on a clinic list","Equal numbers of men and women","Names drawn at random from a registry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: clusters are selected on membership in a group, such as all seniors in support groups or all students in designated classrooms. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A clinic population is 55 percent women and 45 percent men. A sample of 200 patients is drawn by stratified sampling to mirror it. How many women should the sample contain?","choices":["100","90","55","110"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: stratified sampling reproduces the population's makeup, so 55 percent of 200 is 110 women, leaving 90 men. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which study design is the gold standard for evaluating treatment efficacy?","choices":["Prospective cohort study","Retrospective case-control study","Cross-sectional survey","Randomized controlled trial"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because random assignment limits systematic differences between the groups other than the treatment factor, which limits cohort studies. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How do observational findings compare with randomized trial findings?","choices":["They always match the trial findings","They rarely agree with trial findings","They differ only in the size of the sample","They often agree but can differ dramatically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why observational results cannot simply be accepted in place of a randomized trial. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How many of the four possible pairings of study conclusion and reality are errors?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: one error is concluding the treatments differ when they do not, and the other is concluding they do not differ when they do. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"For a randomized trial testing whether a treatment works, what should its primary outcome measure?","choices":["Harms rather than benefits","Cost to the health system","Benefits rather than harms","Compliance with the protocol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the primary outcome must be the benefit of the treatment, not harm, as one of the requirements for an ethical trial. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are inclusion criteria?","choices":["Characteristics participants must have","Factors that prevent participation","Outcomes that count as treatment success","Reasons a participant may later withdraw"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: for example, a diagnosis of the disease being studied; exclusion criteria are the factors that prevent inclusion. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is a treatment?","choices":["A test used to confirm the diagnosis","Only a medication given after diagnosis","An intervention to improve the course of disease","Any exposure that raises the risk of disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it may be a medication, surgery or a lifestyle change, applied at any point in the natural course of disease. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What happens to the pool of eligible patients as more inclusion and exclusion criteria are applied?","choices":["It grows as the population becomes better defined","It shrinks, sometimes to a small fraction","It stays the same because criteria only describe","It shifts entirely into the control group"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: each set of criteria removes more patients, so the final eligible group can be a small fraction of all patients with the condition. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What makes observational treatment studies hard to interpret?","choices":["Excessive control over who receives treatment","Confounding and effect modification","The absence of any real-world patients","Use of blinding that hides the exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: both are difficult to avoid when treatment is not assigned, so differences may reflect other factors rather than the treatment. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is compliance in a trial?","choices":["Agreeing to be randomized to a group","Remaining unaware of the assigned treatment","Acting in accordance with instructions","Having the disease being studied"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is the degree to which participants act in accordance with instructions, or patients with medical advice. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does efficacy represent?","choices":["Results under ordinary real-world circumstances","Results adjusted for compliance and cost","Results that apply to other settings","Results under ideal trial circumstances"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: efficacy is concerned with ideal circumstances, whereas effectiveness reflects ordinary, real circumstances. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
